//  PETPORTS -- PET TREAT
//
//  What the upcycler makes. Nothing consumes it yet: the fuel system is
//  designed and unbuilt, so for now this accumulates in storage and does
//  nothing, which is deliberate and better than the alternative.
//
//  THE OUTPUT EXISTS BEFORE THE CONSUMER ON PURPOSE. An upcycler whose output
//  were nothing at all would be a trash can, and a trash can is what this
//  feature stopped being several design passes ago. A visible pile of Pet
//  Treats is also the failure signal that makes a misconfigured machine
//  survivable -- "why is there 40 of these in here" is a question a player can
//  ask, where a silent absence is not.
//
//  ONE ITEM, NOT A FAMILY, YET. The reagent slot on the upcycler is reserved
//  for a brewing-style second input -- a scorched core making a Spicy Pet
//  Treat, with unit preferences running on flavor rather than on individual
//  item names. That is a V2 branch and this is the stock version it branches
//  from.
//
//  PLACEHOLDER ART: a flat pink square. Not a plain sprite -- an obviously
//  unfinished one, so it cannot be mistaken for a design decision.

{
	"itemName" : "petports_petfuel",
	"price" : 0,
	"rarity" : "Common",
	"category" : "petports_fuel",
	"inventoryIcon" : "petports_petfuel.png",
	"description" : "Reclaimed surplus, pressed into something a utility unit will eat.",
	"shortdescription" : "Pet Treat",
	//  petports_flavor_plain KEEPS THE SET SYMMETRICAL. Every treat carries
	//  exactly one flavor tag and the deposit-beacon subgroups match on
	//  those, so the unflavored one needs its own rather than being the
	//  odd entry matched by name. It also leaves room for the rarity tiers
	//  the flavored treats are set up for: an uncommon plain treat sorts
	//  into the same crate without a manifest edit.
	//  petports_plain_treat IS THE ONE EXCEPTION TO petports_no_upcycling, AND
	//  IT IS A TAG SO THAT IT IS NOT ABOUT THIS ITEM.
	//
	//  Every treat carries petports_no_upcycling so output can never be
	//  laundered back into output. That closes the loop and it also closes the
	//  one conversion that SHOULD happen: a blank treat plus a flavor charge is
	//  a flavored treat, one for one, no points earned and none spent. The
	//  machine made the blank; flavoring it later is finishing the job, not
	//  reprocessing it.
	//
	//  A TAG RATHER THAN A NAME CHECK because a third party adding their own
	//  plain treat should get this for free. The upcycler asks "is this a blank
	//  treat", never "is this petports_petfuel", so a modded blank flavors the
	//  same way with no edit here or there.
	//
	//  FLAVORED TREATS DELIBERATELY DO NOT CARRY IT. They are already finished,
	//  and a spicy treat that could be re-flavored is the laundering loop back
	//  again with an extra step.
	"itemTags" : [ "petports", "petports_fuel", "petports_flavor_plain", "petports_no_upcycling", "petports_plain_treat" ],

	//  PRICE ZERO, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN LAZY.
	//
	//  The upcycler values its input by `price`. A Pet Treat worth anything
	//  would be convertible back into Pet Treats, and the floor of 1 point per
	//  item means even a zero price is not quite zero -- so the upcycler
	//  additionally refuses its own output by name. Both guards, because a
	//  laundering loop that pays out slowly is harder to notice than one that
	//  pays out fast.
	"maxStack" : 1000
}
